              text: 'C ONTEMPORARY I NDIA – II Fig 3.7 B AMBOO D RIP I RRIGATION S YSTEM In Meghalaya, a 200-year -old system of tapping str eam and spring water by using bamboo pipes, is prevalent. About 18-20 litr es of water enters the bamboo pipe system, gets transported over hundreds of metres, and finally reduces to 20-80 drops per minute at the site of the plant. Picture and 3: The channel sections, made of bamboo, divert wate r to the plant site where it is distributed into branches, again made and laid out with different forms of bamboo pipes. The flow of water into the pipes is controlled by manipulating the pipe positions. Picture 1: Bamboo pipes are used to divert perennial springs on the hilltops to the lower reaches by gravity. Picture and Reduced channel sections and diversion units are used at the last stage of water application. The last channel section enables water to be dropped near the roots of the plant. Picture 4: If the pipes pass a road, they are taken high above the land. 1. Collect information on how industries are polluting our water resources. 2. Enact with your classmates a scene of water dispute in your locality . W ATER R ESOURCES E XERCISES E XERCISES E XERCISES E XERCISES E XERCISES 1. 1. 1.1. 1. Multiple choice questions. (i) Based on the information given below classify each of the situations as ‘suffering from water scarcity’ or ‘not suffering from water scarcity’. (a) Region with high annual rainfall. (b) Region having high annual rainfall and large population. (c) Region having high annual rainfall but water is highly polluted.',
